.TH MU2E-SSH-PROBE 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-ssh-probe \- show, or run, the exact ssh command the recovery tools use
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-ssh-probe
.RI [ HOST ...]
.RB [ \-c
.IR CLASS ]
.RB [ \-\-root ]
.RB [ \-\-run
.IR COMMAND ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-chain ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a check reports that a node could not be reached, the next question is
always what command was actually issued, through which gateway, and with which
credential. This prints that command verbatim so it can be pasted into a shell
and debugged by hand, and with
.B \-\-run
executes it and shows what came back.
.PP
.B "Without \-\-run nothing is executed and no connection is opened."
The tool prints the argument vector and the credentials it would try, reports
that nothing was run, and exits 0 \(em whether or not the host would have
answered. A bare invocation is therefore useless as a test of access: use
.B \-\-run true
whenever the question is whether a login succeeds.
.PP
It builds the command through the same code path the phases use \(em the same
jump-host selection, the same
.B ssh
options, the same root login \(em so what it prints is what a real run does.
.SH THE CREDENTIAL CHAIN
By default the probe builds the
.I full
credential chain a real run would try, not just whatever ticket happens to be
in the operator's shell. That distinction is the reason the tool exists in its
present form: with only the ambient ticket, a probe can succeed against a host
the real run cannot reach, which is exactly the confusion it is meant to
prevent.
.PP
The chain is ordered. The operator's own principal is always at its head, for
root sessions as much as for ordinary ones. Only if a host refuses that are the
Mu2e service identities tried \(em
.IR mu2edaq " and " mu2eshift
first, then the remaining identities from Vault. A root session keeps the
.B root
login and varies only the ticket, because a node's
.I root/.k5login
can authorise
.IR mu2edaq ;
.I kerberos.root_fallback
turns that off.
.PP
The login name is taken from
.IR ssh.user " (or " ssh.root_user
for a root session) for the operator's own credential and for every root
credential, and is not derived from the principal. The non-root service
fallbacks are the exception: each carries its own login equal to its identity,
so an ordinary-session fallback logs in as
.IR mu2edaq ", " mu2eshift
and so on whatever
.I ssh.user
says. That is deliberate \(em it is the account the service identity owns \(em
but it means the login can change as the chain advances.
.PP
The chain it builds is the same
.I list
a run builds, but the
.I tickets
are not the same. The probe never calls the run's Kerberos preparation step, so
it mints nothing: the operator credential it reports carries the principal named
by
.I kerberos.principal
while pointing at the
.B ambient
credential cache, and the service identities are listed rather than acquired. A
real run kinits each principal into a cache private to the run and hands
.BR ssh (1)
a
.B KRB5CCNAME
for it. So a line reading
.B "ticket you@FNAL.GOV [ambient cache]"
names the configured principal, not necessarily the one actually in the default
cache \(em confirm that with
.BR klist (1),
or
.B klist \-l
on macOS. There is no option to make the probe mint a ticket.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-run ,
each node's row is followed by the credentials that
.I would
be tried, in order. With
.BR \-\-run ,
the
.B CREDENTIAL
column names the one that actually got in. When every credential is refused,
each login and ticket pair is listed with its own reason rather than collapsed
into one error for the whole chain.
.PP
Before any connection is attempted the probe checks the default credential
cache and warns when it holds a service identity instead of a personal
principal \(em on macOS, where Heimdal makes each newly minted cache the
collection default, that is the usual residue of an earlier run. The warning
carries the
.B "kswitch \-p"
line that restores the operator's own ticket.
.PP
A changed host key is reported as its own failure rather than as a refused
login, and it stops the chain at the first attempt: no identity gets past it.
Verify the new fingerprint out of band \(em from a gateway,
.BI "ssh\-keyscan " node
\(em before updating
.IR ~/.ssh/known_hosts .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I HOST
Nodes to probe, short or fully qualified. Repeatable. Name at least one, or use
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI \-c " CLASS" "\fR, \fP" \-\-class " CLASS"
Probe every node of this host class. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-l " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-location " NAME"
Location to resolve names in.
.TP
.B \-\-root
Use the root login
.RI ( ssh.root_user )
rather than the ordinary one.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " COMMAND"
Actually run this command and show the result. Without it, nothing is executed.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Override the command timeout.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-chain
Use only the ambient ticket and the login
.BR ssh_config (5)
would choose, instead of the credential chain a real run would try. Use it to
answer the narrower question "does my own ticket reach this host", and to
confirm that a difference between the probe and a run is the chain rather than
the network.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the argument vectors and results as JSON, including, for a failure, the
list of attempts with the login, principal and cache of each.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including one line per credential attempted.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
What would be run, and with which credentials, without running it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2e-trk-03
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a mount as root, through the gateway, exactly as the tools would:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2e-trk-03 \-\-root \-\-run 'mountpoint \-q /home; echo $?'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm every tracker node answers at all:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe \-c tracker \-\-run true
.EE
.RE
.PP
Does the run's chain get in where the operator's own ticket does not, or the
other way round?
.RS
.EX
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2egateway01 \-\-run true
mu2e-ssh-probe mu2egateway01 \-\-run true \-\-no\-chain
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every probe succeeded, or nothing was run.
.TP
.B 1
At least one node failed.
.TP
.B 2
No node was named.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2e-power-state (1),
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR kinit (1)
